---
/**
 * foodcraft / CtaBandBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar" CLOSING CTA BAND
 * (design element data-component="CTA-BAND").
 *
 * ROLE. The last ask on the page. One centred ultraviolet slab — a 36px-radius
 * panel filled with --accent — carrying a BLUSH PILL eyebrow, the largest
 * Baloo 2 line after the h1, one sentence, and exactly two ways to act: the
 * owner's own CTA as the cream pill and the owner's own phone number as the
 * paper-outlined pill beside it. It is the same slab the hero opens on, closing
 * the page on the colour it opened with, and it is a CARD on the blush-cream
 * ground rather than a full-bleed band — which is why it opts into none of the
 * wrapper's .band-* classes (see THE GROUND below).
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="CTA-BAND" in
 * "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html":
 *
 *   section    max-width 1200px, centred, padding 76px 28px 0   → .wrap + a
 *              76px top and a ZERO floor (see THE FLOOR)
 *   panel      background #7B4FA8 → --accent; border-radius 36px;
 *              padding 64px 52px; text-align center;
 *              position relative; overflow hidden
 *   eyebrow    #F2C6DA ground → --pink, #5C2A45 label → --plum, 11px / 700 /
 *              .14em caps, padding 8px 16px, radius 100px → --r-pill
 *   h2         Baloo 2 700, clamp(32px,4.6vw,64px), lh 1.06,
 *              margin .24em auto .26em, #FFF7F2 → --on-accent, max-width 20ch
 *   sub        17px / 1.75, #EFE4F6 → --lilac, margin 0 auto 30px,
 *              max-width 42ch
 *   actions    flex, gap 11px, wrap, align-items center, justify-content center
 *   primary    btnCream — which the design's own renderVals() spells
 *              `inline-flex; center; 13px; 700; padding 16px 30px;
 *              border-radius 100px; background #FFF7F2; color #4A2A6B` — i.e.
 *              .btn.btn--cream character for character, with ZERO extra
 *              declarations in this file
 *   secondary  2px solid rgba(255,247,242,.55), #FFF7F2 label, 13px / 700,
 *              padding 15px 28px, radius 100px  → .btn.btn--line re-pointed at
 *              the panel's own on-colour (see THE OUTLINED ACTION)
 *
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE (R1). Every colour the design names
 * already has a wrapper token holding the same value: #7B4FA8 is --accent,
 * #FFF7F2 is --on-accent (and --paper on the cream fill), #F2C6DA is --pink,
 * #5C2A45 is --plum, #4A2A6B is --on-cream, rgba(255,247,242,.55) is
 * rgba(var(--paper-rgb),.55), 100px is --r-pill. The sub-line's #EFE4F6 is the
 * one value with no exact token: it is sRGB 239,228,246 against --lilac's
 * 238,226,245 — one step apart on red and blue, two on green — so it maps to
 * --lilac and no new colour is invented for the difference. What this panel
 * actually renders is --lilac on --accent, which measures 4.77:1 and is clear
 * of the 4.5 floor; #EFE4F6 is never drawn here, so no ratio is quoted for it.
 * HeroBO makes the same mapping for the same pale lilac on the same purple.
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (every one DECLARED in
 *    components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts, so each data-field below is a
 *    hook a real sidebar input owns and focuses) ────────────────────────────
 *   ctaBand.tag       — the blush pill eyebrow (group 'ctaBand', "Eyebrow tag")
 *   ctaBand.headline  — the h2, bound with set:html so an owner's own <em>
 *                       takes this template's colour emphasis wherever THEY put
 *                       it in THEIR sentence
 *   ctaBand.sub       — the one sentence under it
 *   ctaBand.cta.text  — the cream action's label
 *     + data-href-field ctaBand.cta.href — its destination
 *   location.phone    — the outlined action (data-field ONLY, see THE PHONE)
 * Nothing else. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook that no sidebar
 * input owns and that focuses nothing, so no other path is written here.
 *
 * ── DERIVED, never stored and never editable ────────────────────────────────
 *   · the CTA's href, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBO actually
 *     mounts, and its target/rel pair — one conditional per attribute, so
 *     neither is ever emitted twice
 *   · the tel: href, built from the phone field's OWN digits
 *
 * ── NOT SUPPLIED BY THIS COMPONENT (R2) ─────────────────────────────────────
 * NOT ONE VISIBLE WORD. The design's demo strings — "Thirsty yet?", "Send your
 * order, skip the line." and "Message us your drinks and a pickup time…" — are
 * the DESIGNER filling in their own template. A real shop gets a closing band
 * that says only what that shop said, so every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag: no
 * blush pill over nothing, no display line above an empty panel, and no action
 * row with no actions in it.
 *
 * ── NOT RENDERED, deliberately ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   · ctaBand.image. THE DESIGN GIVES THIS BAND NO PHOTOGRAPH — it is a flat
 *     ultraviolet slab, four centred elements, nothing else. The schema declares
 *     the field because other templates' closing bands are full-bleed pictures;
 *     here it stays in the sidebar and is simply unused, which is the honest
 *     outcome. Drawing a .plate nobody asked for would put a grey slab in the
 *     middle of the one section of this sheet that is pure colour, and every
 *     contrast figure below is measured against the flat --accent, which an
 *     arbitrary upload underneath would make unmeasurable. HeroBO and GalleryBO
 *     already give an owner's photographs a home on this page.
 *   · No DERIVED emphasis on the headline. HeroBO turns the tail of the h1
 *     blush because the design's h1 IS two-tone and no owner would type a tag
 *     into a text box. This design's h2 is drawn one flat cream — it has no
 *     colour turn — so deriving one here would be inventing a treatment the
 *     design does not draw. An owner's OWN <em> is still honoured, and is
 *     re-pointed at the blush so it cannot land purple-on-purple (see R3).
 *   · No id on the <section>. This page's anchors are menu / about / gallery /
 *     visit / faq / main; the design gives CTA-BAND none of them and no nav link
 *     points at it, so inventing one would add a target nothing aims for. A
 *     derived '#book' resolves to '#main' through ANCHOR_ALIAS below.
 *   · No aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote; the h2 already titles the region.
 *   · No star row, no rating, no count, no figure of any kind. Nothing in this
 *     band is a claim, and the ctaBand group declares no numeric field to draw
 *     one from honestly, so there is nothing here to fabricate.
 *   · No role="img" and no .plate anywhere — this section holds no upload, so
 *     there is nothing to give an image role to and nothing to name.
 *   · No target="_blank" on the cream action unless the href is really off-site.
 *     The design hard-codes one because its demo href is an external messaging
 *     URL, but the same button resolves to '#visit' on a stock build and opening
 *     an in-page anchor in a new tab is a bug. It is emitted for http(s) ONLY.
 *   · No .lede / .body primitive on the sub-line. Both are --ink-soft, a
 *     paper-ground ink that is 1.27:1 against this purple — it is DARKER than
 *     the panel, so the pair is not merely low but effectively unreadable;
 *     opting in and then overriding colour, size, leading, measure and margin
 *     would be more code, not less. The sub-line is written out instead.
 *
 * ── THE GROUND, and why no .band-* class ────────────────────────────────────
 * PageBO declares .band-pink, .band-sage and .band-lilac, each carrying its own
 * text colours, and a section should reach for one of those rather than paint a
 * background and then pick a text colour by hand. None of them applies here.
 * The design's closing ground is #7B4FA8 — the PURPLE, for which the wrapper
 * declares no band class, because in this design the purple is never a
 * full-width band: it is a rounded CARD floating on the blush-cream page, in the
 * hero and again here. So the <section> itself stays on --paper and takes no
 * band class at all, and the panel declares the --accent / --on-accent pair
 * TOGETHER — exactly the way the wrapper's own band rules declare theirs — so
 * that nothing inside it has to guess what is legible. FOR REVIEW: if a
 * .band-accent (or a shared `sip-panel`) is ever added to PageBO, this file and
 * HeroBO should both move onto it; they are currently the only two declaring
 * that pair.
 *
 * ── THE OUTLINED ACTION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * .btn--line is the wrapper's outlined button and both its edge and its label
 * are --accent, which is invisible on an --accent panel, so both are re-pointed
 * at the panel's on-colour and the edge is widened to the design's 2px.
 * THE TWO PILLS ARE THE SAME HEIGHT, by two routes: 15px + a 2px border is 17px
 * and so is the cream pill's 16px + 1px; and in any case both boxes come to a
 * 13px line box + 34px of vertical inset = 47px and are lifted to .btn's own
 * 48px min-height. HORIZONTALLY THEY DIFFER BY ONE PIXEL — 28 + 2 against
 * 30 + 1 — which is the design's own difference (its cream pill carries no
 * border at all) and is kept rather than equalised.
 *
 * ── THE PHONE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * THE PHONE ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY — no data-href-field. Its href is
 * DERIVED from the field's own value, so a data-href-field there would write
 * "tel:+63…" back into the phone field on the first inline edit; V3 also bails
 * out of inline editing on tel:/mailto: anchors and on anything carrying
 * data-href-field. This is the family's single documented exception to "every
 * anchor gets both".
 *
 * ITS READ CHAIN is content.location.phone || content.contact.phone, which is
 * exactly what the sidebar's Location → Phone input reads (the schema declares
 * location.phone with fallbackPaths ['contact.phone'] and writes back to the
 * primary), so the rendered number and the field this hook opens are always the
 * same value. layout.contact.phone — the display-formatted mirror the builder
 * derives — is deliberately NOT preferred: astro-builder formats
 * content.contact.phone and ignores location.phone entirely, so on a site whose
 * owner typed a number into Location it would render a DIFFERENT number from
 * the one the hook edits.
 *
 * WHY A location.* HOOK IS SAFE HERE. editorBridge resolves a Blocks toggle to
 * the ONE section holding the most hooks of that prefix and hides only that
 * node. LocationBO carries location.tag, location.headline and location.hours
 * and says in as many words that "the closing band and the footer own" the
 * phone, so this band's single location.* hook cannot outvote it and "hide
 * LOCATION" keeps resolving to the Location section.
 *
 * ── SECTION GATE (R5) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The band renders when it has BODY content: an ACTION (the CTA label or a
 * phone number) or the SENTENCE. It deliberately does NOT count the eyebrow or
 * the headline — lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills ctaBand.headline with
 * "Ready when you are." on EVERY site, so a gate that counted it would be a
 * no-op that ships a display line over an empty purple slab. derive also fills
 * ctaBand.cta.text ('Get in touch') unconditionally, which is why the band does
 * in practice render on a stock build — but that is a real, working action
 * pointed at a real id on this page, not a decoration, and an owner who clears
 * it and supplies no phone and no sentence drops the whole <section> from the
 * document rather than being left with one line of type on a purple card.
 * (derive fills neither ctaBand.tag nor ctaBand.sub, so both are genuinely
 * absent until an owner types them.)
 *
 * ── THE FLOOR ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The design's section is `padding: 76px 28px 0` — a 76px top and NO bottom —
 * because in that file the FOOTER element carries its own `padding: 56px 28px
 * 40px`. The wrapper's shared `section` rule is a symmetric
 * clamp(48px,6.4vw,80px), so the bottom is zeroed here rather than inherited.
 * This is the one value in the file that depends on a sibling, so it was CHECKED
 * rather than left for review: FooterBO's `.sip-foot` declares
 * `padding: 56px 0 40px` — the design's 56px top, intact — and steps to
 * `40px 0 28px` at 520px, where this band's own top also steps back to 56px. The
 * pair is verified in both passes. If FooterBO's top padding is ever removed,
 * the floor has to come back here.
 *
 * ── ACCESSIBILITY (R9) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The tel: link's accessible name is the owner's own number and nothing else —
 * no "Call …" prefix I wrote. The cream action's name is the owner's own label.
 * There is no image, no icon and no decorative numeral in this section, so
 * nothing here is aria-hidden and no aria-* attribute is emitted at all — which
 * also means there is no conditional aria-* that a literal one could collide
 * with. FOCUS: the wrapper's ring is a --paper outline inside an --accent halo,
 * built for the paper ground; on this panel the halo would be accent-on-accent
 * and the cream outline would vanish into the cream pill's own fill, so the two
 * colours swap — the same move the wrapper already makes for .band-pink. Only
 * the colours change; the 3px/6px geometry and the pill radius stay the
 * wrapper's.
 *
 * ── CONTRAST, COMPUTED (sRGB relative luminance, WCAG 2.x) against this
 *    panel's own ground rather than estimated ─────────────────────────────
 *   --on-accent headline / outlined label on --accent    5.63:1
 *   --lilac sub-line on --accent                         4.77:1
 *   --plum label on the --pink pill                      7.41:1
 *   --on-cream label on the cream pill (--paper)        10.81:1 — the label
 *       sits on the cream fill, not on the panel, so the purple does not
 *       change it
 * Every one of those clears AA at any size. Only the FIRST of the four appears
 * in PageBO's own measured table, as "paper on an accent fill 5.63" — --paper
 * and --on-accent are the same hex, so it is literally the same measurement.
 * PageBO lists neither --lilac on --accent nor --on-cream on --paper at all,
 * and for the blush pill it measures a DIFFERENT pair than the one this design
 * draws (--on-pink on --pink, 9.96, where the design's eyebrow label is --plum).
 * Those three are therefore computed in this file rather than borrowed.
 *
 * ONE NON-TEXT PAIR TO REVIEW, stated the same way HeroBO states it because it
 * is literally the same edge. The outlined pill's border is the design's own
 * `2px solid rgba(paper,.55)`, which resolves to sRGB 196,171,209 over the panel
 * and measures 2.86:1 against it — just under the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 asks of a UI
 * component boundary. The design is the specification, so the value is
 * reproduced rather than quietly nudged; the control is still identifiable by
 * its label, which is --on-accent at 5.63:1. Raising the alpha to .60 gives
 * 3.12:1 and is a one-character change if a reviewer wants the bar cleared —
 * but it must then be changed in HeroBO too, or the page's two ghost buttons
 * stop matching.
 *
 * ── CLASS-NAME LAW (R4, not inferable, do not "tidy") ───────────────────────
 * The section is .sip-end, NEVER .cta-band: lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts emits
 * `.cta-band .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }` for every state and would
 * strand both labels here. Buttons are .btn + .btn--cream / .btn--line (double
 * dash) for the same reason. Never .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow /
 * .btn-light / .nav-cta / .nav-right / .trust / .testi in this family, and never
 * `.hero` on a <section>.
 * The prefix is `sip-` — this template's own ("Sipsip", and the design's own id
 * on its hero image slot), shared with HeroBO's `sip-hero-*` because that file
 * draws the SAME purple panel. It IS uniform across BO — all fifteen section
 * components, HeaderBO through FooterBO, prefix `sip-` and nothing else — and an
 * earlier draft of this block described a split the files do not have. Every
 * class here is Astro-scoped, so the prefix costs nothing at runtime either
 * way. What matters is that no name is carried over from ANOTHER TEMPLATE: BL
 * uses `kc-`, BM uses `tc-`, BN uses `pd-`, and none of those appears here — copying a
 * sibling's class names verbatim is the mistake an audit had to unpick on an
 * earlier template.
 *
 * ── R3 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * One scoped <style> block, no is:global. The headline is bound with set:html,
 * and elements created that way cannot be reached by an ordinary scoped rule —
 * so the owner's <em> is styled through :global(). It NEEDS that rule: PageBO's
 * `:where(.h2 em) { color: var(--accent) }` would paint the emphasis purple on a
 * purple panel and make it disappear. It becomes --pink instead, and font-style
 * is pinned to normal because an em is italic by user-agent default and Baloo 2
 * ships no italic — the browser would synthesise an oblique that fights its
 * rounded terminals. The blush is 3.93:1 on the purple, which is the large-text
 * bar (3:1) and not the body bar; the h2 is 700 weight and never drops below
 * 26px in any query below, so it stays large text at every width.
 * SPECIFICITY: PageBO wraps its primitives in :where(), so those rules score
 * (0,1,1) — the `html[data-page="BO"]` prefix and nothing from the :where().
 * An Astro-scoped rule here scores at least (0,2,0) and therefore wins even
 * INSIDE the wrapper's own media queries, which is why the outlined button's
 * side padding is restated at 520px rather than left to inherit the wrapper's
 * narrow pass.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// ── ANCHOR GUARD (R7) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Copied from HeroBO, character for character, and pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes ctaBand.cta.href = '#visit'; this
// page DOES draw '#visit', so on a stock build the fallback never fires — but
// an owner (or a future derive) can type any of the aliases below, so resolve
// every in-page href against what PageBO ACTUALLY mounts: keep it when the id
// exists, remap it when this design carries the same block under another name,
// otherwise fall back to the target the design was built around. Off-page hrefs
// (tel:, mailto:, http and anything else without a leading '#') pass through
// untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBO — and with the copies
// in HeaderBO, HeroBO and FooterBO. There is no shared import by design (a
// section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the
// copies must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  location: 'visit', contact: 'visit',
  services: 'menu', rooms: 'menu',
  host: 'about', why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
  book: 'main',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[href.slice(1)];
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(href.slice(1))) return href;
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const c = (content as any) || {};
const cta = c.ctaBand || {};

// `||`, never `??`: an owner who CLEARS a box leaves '' behind, and the sidebar
// treats '' as empty too. With `??` the empty string would count as a value and
// the element would render blank instead of being dropped.
const tag = String(cta.tag || '').trim();
const headline = String(cta.headline || '').trim();
const sub = String(cta.sub || '').trim();

const ctaText = String(cta.cta?.text || '').trim();
// '#visit' is the fallback because that is where this design puts the address,
// the map and the hours — the thing a closing ask points at.
const ctaHref = onPage(cta.cta?.href, '#visit');
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// See THE PHONE in the doc comment for why this exact read chain.
const phone = String(c.location?.phone || c.contact?.phone || '').trim();
const telHref = phone ? `tel:${phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '')}` : '';

const hasActions = Boolean(ctaText || phone);
// SECTION GATE (R5) — body content only: an action or the sentence. Neither the
// eyebrow nor the headline counts; see the doc comment.
const hasBand = Boolean(hasActions || sub);
---
{hasBand && (
<section class="sip-end">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sip-end-panel reveal">
      {/* The eyebrow's face, weight, size, caps and inline-block come from the
          shared .kick; what is restated below is everything that turns an
          eyebrow into a blush pill — plus the label colour, because .kick's
          default is --accent and purple on the blush is 3.93:1, the one pairing
          this palette must never make. */}
      {tag && <span class="kick sip-end-kick" data-field="ctaBand.tag">{tag}</span>}
      {/* set:html so the owner's own <em> lands where THEY put it in THEIR
          sentence. The colour change is re-pointed at the blush below (R3). */}
      {headline && <h2 class="h2 sip-end-h" data-field="ctaBand.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
      {sub && <p class="sip-end-sub" data-field="ctaBand.sub">{sub}</p>}
      {hasActions && (
        <div class="sip-end-acts">
          {ctaText && (
            <a
              class="btn btn--cream"
              href={ctaHref}
              target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
              rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
              data-field="ctaBand.cta.text"
              data-href-field="ctaBand.cta.href"
            >{ctaText}</a>
          )}
          {/* data-field ONLY — no data-href-field. See THE PHONE. */}
          {phone && (
            <a class="btn btn--line sip-end-tel" href={telHref} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBO's token vocabulary
     (--accent --on-accent --paper --paper-rgb --pink --plum --lilac --on-cream
     --disp --r-pill) and from its shared primitives (.wrap .kick .h2 .btn
     .btn--cream .btn--line .reveal). There is no literal hex in this file. The
     two translucent values are written against --paper-rgb, which
     genericThemeOverrides owns and repaints in step with --paper (both come from
     the same palette entry), so the outlined pill's edge follows the page's own
     ground instead of freezing at a literal. On THIS palette that is also the
     panel's on-colour — --paper and --on-accent are the same hex — but the engine
     derives --on-accent by luminance (pickOn) rather than from --paper, so under
     a repainted scheme the two can part company. The edge is decorative; the
     label carries the contrast either way.

     (Never write a literal end-style-tag inside this block, comments included —
      it is a raw-text element, so the parser would end the stylesheet there and
      silently truncate the rest of the file.) */

  /* Design: max-width:1200px; margin:0 auto; padding:76px 28px 0. The measure
     and the gutter are .wrap's job — the same substitution HeroBO makes for the
     same 1200/28 box, so the two purple panels on this page stay the same
     width. The 76px top and the ZERO floor are this section's; see THE FLOOR. */
  .sip-end { padding: 76px 0 0; }

  /* Design: background --accent; border-radius:36px; padding:64px 52px;
     text-align:center; position:relative; overflow:hidden.
     36px is a length, not a colour: this design's panel corner is softer than
     --r-lg (26px), which is the card radius everywhere else on the page, and
     HeroBO states the same 36px for the same slab.
     position/overflow are the design's own and are kept: they clip the panel's
     children to that radius, which is what keeps a long owner CTA label from
     poking a square corner out of a round one at the narrow end.
     The ground/ink pair is declared TOGETHER for the same reason the wrapper's
     band rules declare theirs — so nothing inside has to guess. */
  .sip-end-panel {
    background-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--on-accent);
    border-radius: 36px;
    padding: 64px 52px;
    text-align: center;
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Design: background --pink; color --plum; font-size:11px; font-weight:700;
     letter-spacing:.14em; text-transform:uppercase; padding:8px 16px;
     border-radius:100px. Font size and caps come from .kick; its .18em tracking
     and its --accent ink do not, and are replaced.
     The pill centres by itself — .kick is inline-block inside a text-align
     centred panel — so no margin is invented for it. max-width + overflow-wrap
     keep an owner-typed tag in tracked caps wrapping INSIDE the pill instead of
     widening the panel (R8).
     The wrapper's own 520 pass takes .kick to 10.5px/.14em; only the tracking is
     restated here and at the same value, so nothing in this rule fights it. */
  .sip-end-kick {
    background-color: var(--pink);
    color: var(--plum);
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: Baloo 2 700, clamp(32px,4.6vw,64px), lh 1.06,
     margin .24em auto .26em, --on-accent, max-width 20ch. Face and weight are
     .h2's; size, leading, margin, colour and measure are all larger/other than
     that primitive's, so all five are stated. The `auto` side margins are what
     centre a 20ch block inside the panel.
     THE MEASURE IS KEPT BELOW 760px on purpose. The wrapper's 760 pass releases
     the shared .h2 to `max-width:none`, which is right for a left-aligned
     heading; this one is centred, and a centred display line allowed to run the
     full panel loses the stacked shape the design draws. A scoped rule outranks
     that pass, so the 20ch simply stays. */
  .sip-end-h {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.6vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.06;
    margin: .24em auto .26em;
    color: var(--on-accent);
    max-width: 20ch;
  }
  /* THE OWNER'S OWN EMPHASIS. :global() because Astro cannot scope markup that
     arrived through set:html (R3). It is a COLOUR CHANGE and NOT an italic:
     font-style is pinned to normal because an em is italic by user-agent
     default and Baloo 2 has no italic to serve. Without this rule PageBO's
     `.h2 em` would paint it --accent — purple on a purple panel. */
  .sip-end-h :global(em) { font-style: normal; color: var(--pink); }

  /* Design: font-size:17px; line-height:1.75; a pale lilac; margin:0 auto 30px;
     max-width:42ch. Written out rather than borrowing .lede or .body — see NOT
     RENDERED. `auto` side margins centre the measure inside the panel; the 42ch
     is a cap, so on any viewport where the panel is narrower than 42ch it
     simply does not bind and never pushes anything sideways. */
  .sip-end-sub {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--lilac);
    margin: 0 auto 30px;
    max-width: 42ch;
  }

  /* Design: display:flex; gap:11px; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center;
     justify-content:center. */
  .sip-end-acts {
    display: flex;
    gap: 11px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* Design: border:2px solid the paper at 55%; color --on-accent;
     font-size:13px; font-weight:700; padding:15px 28px; border-radius:100px.
     .btn--line's edge and label are both --accent — invisible on an --accent
     panel — so both are re-pointed at the panel's on-colour. Only the width,
     the two colours and the padding are set: the solid border style and the
     pill radius come from .btn's own shorthand. This is the file's ONLY button
     declaration; the cream primary needs none at all, because btnCream and
     .btn--cream are the same declaration list. */
  .sip-end-tel {
    background-color: transparent;
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .55);
    color: var(--on-accent);
    padding: 15px 28px;
  }
  /* .btn--line's hover FILLS with --accent, which on this panel would make the
     button vanish into its own ground. The replacement brings the edge to full
     strength and adds the lightest possible wash: at .08 the ground resolves to
     sRGB 134,92,174 and the --on-accent label still measures 4.77:1; past about
     .10 it drops under 4.5, so this is not a value to nudge upward for a
     stronger effect. Same figure, same reasoning, same value as HeroBO's ghost. */
  .sip-end-tel:hover {
    background-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .08);
    border-color: var(--on-accent);
    color: var(--on-accent);
  }

  /* THE FOCUS RING, INVERTED FOR THIS PANEL — see ACCESSIBILITY. Colours only;
     the geometry and the pill radius stay the wrapper's. */
  .sip-end-acts .btn:focus-visible {
    outline-color: var(--accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 6px var(--on-accent);
  }

  /* ── R8 · this section's OWN responsive pass ───────────────────────────────
     There is no multi-column grid here to collapse — the design's closing band
     is one centred stack at every width — so what this section owns is the
     panel's inset and its type ladder, plus making sure nothing can push the
     page sideways at 360px.

     THE LADDER, traced at EVERY boundary rather than assumed, because two
     audits found type and plates that GREW as the viewport narrowed (a floor in
     the 900 query sitting below a cap in the 520 query is how that happens):

       h2 font-size
         base clamp(32,4.6vw,64) · 900 clamp(30,4.6vw,42) · 520 clamp(26,6.4vw,30)
         at 900px  base gives 4.6vw = 41.4px (inside 32–64) -> 41.4px;
                   this query gives min(max(30,41.4),42) = 41.4px. EQUAL.
         at 520px  the 900 query gives 4.6vw = 23.92 -> floored to 30px;
                   the 520 query gives 6.4vw = 33.28 -> capped to 30px. EQUAL —
                   which is why the cap here is 30 and not the 34 a fresh
                   reading of "smaller than 42" would suggest.
         at 360px  6.4vw = 23.04 -> floored to 26px. Down, never up.
         26px at weight 700 is still WCAG "large text" (the bar is 18.66px
         bold), which is what keeps the blush em's 3.93:1 above its 3:1 floor at
         the narrowest width.

       panel block padding
         base flat 64px · 900 clamp(48,7vw,58) · 520 clamp(40,9vw,48)
         at 900px  base 64px; this query gives 7vw = 63 -> capped to 58px. DOWN.
         at 520px  the 900 query gives 7vw = 36.4 -> floored to 48px;
                   the 520 query gives 9vw = 46.8 (inside 40–48) -> 46.8px. DOWN.
                   The 48px cap is exactly the handover value, so it can never
                   grow through the boundary.
         at 360px  9vw = 32.4 -> floored to 40px. Down.

       panel inline padding
         base flat 52px · 900 clamp(28,5vw,44) · 520 clamp(20,6vw,28)
         at 900px  base 52px; this query gives 5vw = 45 -> capped to 44px. DOWN.
         at 520px  the 900 query gives 5vw = 26 -> floored to 28px;
                   the 520 query gives 6vw = 31.2 -> capped to 28px. EQUAL.
         at 360px  6vw = 21.6px. Down.

       sub-line     17 -> 16.5 -> 16.   Flat values, trivially monotonic.
       eyebrow      11 -> 11 -> 10.5.   The 10.5 is the wrapper's own 520 pass,
                                        not restated here.
       section top  76 -> 76 -> 56.     Flat, and the one step is downward. The
                                        gap above the panel is the design's own
                                        76px until the phone widths, where 76px
                                        of empty blush above a 40px-inset card
                                        reads as a hole rather than as air.
     Every step is a step down and every handover is continuous. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .sip-end-panel { padding: clamp(48px, 7vw, 58px) clamp(28px, 5vw, 44px); }
    .sip-end-h { font-size: clamp(30px, 4.6vw, 42px); }
    .sip-end-sub { font-size: 16.5px; }
  }

  /* Nothing may push the page sideways at 360px: long unbroken strings break
     anywhere, and a long label wraps inside a full-width button instead of
     widening the row. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .sip-end { padding-top: 56px; }
    .sip-end-panel { padding: clamp(40px, 9vw, 48px) clamp(20px, 6vw, 28px); }
    .sip-end-h {
      font-size: clamp(26px, 6.4vw, 30px);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .sip-end-sub {
      font-size: 16px;
      margin-bottom: 26px;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .sip-end-acts {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      gap: 11px;
    }
    .sip-end-acts .btn {
      width: 100%;
      /* BOTH are needed. `white-space: normal` only lets an owner's label wrap
         BETWEEN words; one long unbroken word still spills past a 100%-wide
         button on a 360px screen without a break of its own — an audit found
         exactly that escape because only the first was set. */
      white-space: normal;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
      text-align: center;
    }
    /* Re-join the wrapper's own 520 pass, which takes .btn to 15px 24px. A
       scoped rule here scores above that EVEN INSIDE the wrapper's media query,
       so without this the outlined pill would keep its 28px sides while the
       cream one dropped to 24px. 22px takes 6px off the base 28px — the same 6px
       the wrapper takes off the cream pill's 30px — so at 22/24 the pair keeps
       exactly the 2px padding difference the design gives it at 28/30, and the
       one-pixel outer difference their borders make (22+2 = 24 against
       24+1 = 25) is the same one pixel they carry at full width (28+2 = 30
       against 30+1 = 31). HeroBO's ghost drops to the same 22px for the same
       reason. The vertical stays 15px, which is what the wrapper's narrow pass
       uses anyway. */
    .sip-end-tel {
      padding-left: 22px;
      padding-right: 22px;
    }
  }
</style>
